intent: >
  Build the referral landing page Steve's tree-service partner can link
  homeowners to directly after a large tree comes out, so the referral
  funnel has a single high-conversion target rather than dropping
  homeowners on the generic homepage or service pages.

slice: 2/3
kind: feature
behavior_change: additive

reversibility: trivial

scope:
  declared:
    - src/after-tree-removal.njk
  actual:
    - src/after-tree-removal.njk
  drift: []

proof:
  tests_run: "Manual: dev server already running; navigated to /after-tree-removal/ via Chrome. Verified 200 response, page title, meta description, all 4 H2s render in correct order, 8 H3s (4 problem cards + 4 solution cards), 4 internal links to /irrigation-systems/ and /plant-selection/, TODO Steve markers stripped from served HTML, zero em-dashes in served HTML"
  result: green
  failing: 0

tests:
  needed: "no"
  rationale: >
    Static Nunjucks template, no logic. Verified by rendering in dev
    server and DOM inspection. Eleventy build was clean (no template
    errors, page resolved at /after-tree-removal/ as expected by the
    file-based routing).

assumptions:
  - "Steve performs all four restoration services himself (irrigation, lawn, soil, replanting). Locked decision from the plan; if Steve actually subs out lawn restoration or soil work, the relevant card needs softening from 'Steve does X' to 'Steve coordinates X'."
  - "The /after-tree-removal/ URL is what the tree-service partner will link to. Not in main nav by design (referral page, not browse-able service page). Discoverable only via direct link."
  - "Internal links to /irrigation-systems/ and /plant-selection/ resolve in production. Verified on dev server."

specifications:
  - "Page refuses to be a generic landscape sales pitch. Speaks to a specific homeowner moment (yard just got torn up by a tree removal crew)."
  - "Hero opens with the homeowner's situation, not Steve's credentials. Validation before solution."
  - "Problem section (what tree removal breaks) is educational, not alarmist. 4 specific impact areas with concrete detail (drip lines, soil compaction, root cavity, sun exposure)."
  - "Solution section (what Steve restores) explicitly attributes the hands-on work to Steve, not to a generic 'Lytle team'. Concierge spine."
  - "Concierge framing paragraph between solution and CTA states the body plan out loud: one person walks the yard rather than five subs."
  - "Dual CTA at bottom (form + phone) matches the pattern on service-area pages."

deviations_from_plan: >
  Plan said hero would carry the concierge framing in the H1. Implementation
  has the concierge framing in the hero sub-paragraph and a dedicated
  section between solution and CTA. Lighter touch in the hero, heavier
  later. Same content, different placement, more scannable.

  Also: first pass shipped two em-dashes in title and meta description.
  Caught them during browser verification and replaced with commas before
  the commit. Worth flagging because the writing analysis explicitly flags
  em-dashes as AI tells; I should be running a grep for them before every
  template change going forward.

trade_offs: >
  Chose to launch the page without a hero image. Steve can add a torn-up-
  yard or restoration-in-progress photo later. Cost: page is text-heavy
  on first impression. Benefit: doesn't block ship on a photo Steve may
  not have. Alternative would have been to reuse an existing portfolio
  shot, but none of his existing images depict the specific moment this
  page speaks to.

  Also chose NOT to add this URL to the main nav or any nav. It's a
  referral landing target, not a service page, and putting it in the nav
  would dilute the homepage's homeowner-focused services list. Discoverable
  only via direct link from the tree-service partner (and crawled by search
  engines via the sitemap).

interfaces:
  consumed:
    - "/#contact form (existing) — page CTAs link here"
    - "/irrigation-systems/ (existing) — internal link in restoration card"
    - "/plant-selection/ (existing) — internal link in restoration card"

self_review:
  consistency: >
    Markup pattern matches existing service-area pages and the concierge
    section from slice 1 (PR #10): same Tailwind grid/card vocabulary,
    same icon + h3 + p shape inside cards, same bg-alternation between
    sections.
  metz: N/A — Nunjucks template.
  body_plan: >
    Page has one job: convert a homeowner who just lost a tree. It refuses
    to know about other services Steve offers, other service areas, or
    Steve's broader credentials. Those live elsewhere. Drawable: 5 sections
    in a single column down the page, each section earning its place.
  honesty: >
    Voice is best-guess Steve. TODO marker is in source. The hands-on
    attribution ('Steve finds the cut lines, repairs them...') is load-
    bearing on the locked plan decision. If that decision was wrong, the
    page needs softening — flagging in assumptions.

confidence: high
reviewer_attention:
  - "src/after-tree-removal.njk:53-76 (the 'What Steve restores' cards) — these explicitly attribute the work to Steve personally. If Steve subs any of this out, the relevant card needs rewording"
  - "src/after-tree-removal.njk:81-87 (concierge spine) — states the body plan out loud. Worth a sanity check that the framing matches how Steve actually pitches against multi-vendor alternatives"
